# howto.psv -- how to actually RUN a program, where that is not obvious.
#
#   <name>|<one line, imperative>
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. A play-test of the collection found that most programs
# reported as "does nothing" or "just goes dead" were working perfectly and
# waiting for an argument, a data file or a working directory that nothing on
# the disk mentioned. `orbit' is the case that made the point: it prints
# '"kepler.dat" not found' and exits, and kepler.dat was on the disk the whole
# time, in DOC/orbit. Run it from there and it offers you 21 satellites.
#
# Most programs need no entry: gen_catalog.py lifts the usage text out of the
# binary itself for 266 of them, and that is better than anything written by
# hand because it cannot go stale. This file is for what the binary does NOT
# say -- where the data lives, which program feeds which, why a thing dies.
#
# Keep each line to one sentence or two. It is read at an OS-9 prompt, in 80
# columns, by someone who has just been disappointed.

orbit|Reads a bare "kepler.dat" from the CURRENT directory, so run it from where that file is: `chd /dd/DOC/orbit` first. Then it lists 21 satellites and asks which.
nasa|The program that FEEDS orbit. Give it NASA two-line elements in a file called nasa.dat in the current directory and it writes kepler.dat, which is what orbit reads. Neither file ships -- you supply nasa.dat.
pwgen|pwgen <length> [count]. With no arguments it prints nothing and exits, which reads as a hang and is not one.
bmgtest|bmgtest [-i] [-n] <pattern> [file ...]. A demonstration of Boyer-Moore-Gosper searching, not a tool you would use.
life|life [init-file]. The patterns are in /dd/GAMES/LIFE -- try `life /dd/GAMES/LIFE/glider`. It also wants more memory than the default; from the OS-9 shell that is `life #22k <file>`, and bash has no #size syntax at all.
bio|BASIC09 I-code, not 68000 code. `load runb` first, then run `bio` by BARE NAME. Giving runb a pathname instead raises BASIC09 error 43, which reads like a broken program and is not.
logisim|Simulates a logic circuit described in a file. The format is in DOC/logisim/logisim.doc; there is no example circuit on the disk.
calender|Prints the year in GERMAN. Not a typo of `calendar' -- a different program by a different author.
gdd|A data dump -- like `od', with GNU-style long options. Needs Microware's cio.
easter|Prints Easter dates for 1988 to 2000 and nothing else. The range is compiled in.
england|One of six weather simulators that differ only in climate and calendar: england, florida, georgia, minnesota, japan (Japanese calendar) and shire (Middle-earth). Each prints a day's weather and stops.
japan|A weather simulator, not a calendar tool -- see `england'. It uses the Japanese calendar, which is the only reason it looks like one.
shire|A weather simulator using the Middle-earth calendar -- see `england'.
sokoban|Wants a username, so run it from a login rather than a bare shell, or it stops with "cannot get your username".
robots|Play with `robots -m' -- manual mode, where the robots take one step per move you make. Keys are the numeric keypad 1-9 (5 stands still), `s' for last stand, `t' to teleport. Needs Microware's math module and a real TERM.
advint|Plays an ADVSYS .adi world file. THERE IS NO WORLD FILE ON THIS DISK, so it has nothing to do until you write one with advcom.
advcom|Compiles ADVSYS .adv source into a .adi world for advint. No .adv source ships here either -- this pair is for writing adventures, not playing them.
infocom|A Z-machine. Plays the .z3 files in /dd/GAMES/INFORM, which are Inform demonstration programs (dejavu, hellow, shell), not the Infocom games.
advent|Colossal Cave. Needs this disk as /dd -- it opens /dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz by absolute path, so mounted only as /h0 it cannot find its data.
pbmtext|pbmtext <word> draws it as an image. `pbmtext os9 | pbmtoascii' prints it on the terminal and needs no file at all -- the shortest demonstration of the 169 NETPBM programs. See DOC/README-NETPBM.
pbmtoascii|Prints an image as characters, so NETPBM can be seen on an ordinary terminal with no graphics. Try `pnminvert /dd/DEMO/sphere.pgm | pgmtopbm -threshold -value 0.5 | pbmtoascii'.
pnmscale|pnmscale <factor> <file>. Given only a filename it takes THAT as the factor and then reads empty standard input, reporting "bad magic number" -- which means you left out the factor, not that anything is broken.
pcxtoppm|Cannot read a pipe: it seeks backwards in its input and stops with "error seeking past header". Write the PCX to a file first and pass the filename.
sgitopnm|Cannot read a pipe -- same as pcxtoppm. Give it a filename or it reports "premature EOF".
yuvtoppm|yuvtoppm <width> <height>. The dimensions are not stored in a YUV file, so you must supply the ones ppmtoyuv started from.
lispmtopgm|This build handles at most 16 grey levels and says "depth is too large" otherwise. Run the image through `pnmdepth 15' before pgmtolispm.
pbmtopk|A TeX font tool, not an image converter: it wants a pkfile, a .tfm metric file and a resolution. No .tfm ships on this disk.
nroff|Formats man pages. The -man macros in LIB/tmac.an were extended for this collection because the originals defined only .TH .SH .SS .PP and .I; LIB/orig.tmac.an is the untouched version. Try `nroff -man /dd/DOC/netpbm/pnmscale.1'.
lua|Lua 3.0, and it needs Microware's csl -- see DOC/README-CIO. Run a script with `lua file.lua'. NOTE: 3.0 has no numeric `for' loop; that arrived in Lua 3.1, so `for i=1,10 do' is a syntax error here and `while' is the idiom. Examples in DOC/lua/examples.
luac|Compiles a Lua script to bytecode: `luac -o out in.lua'. Needs csl. runc then runs the result as an OS-9 command.
gnuplot|Type `set term' first -- it lists every output device it knows, and refuses to plot until you choose one. Its whole manual is built in: type `help'. Demos and sample data are in DOC/gnuplot/demo. Needs Microware's cio.
gawk|GNU awk 2.11, the first awk this disk has ever carried. Needs Microware's cio. `gawk "{print \$1}" file' -- and mind that the OS-9 shell, not gawk, is what mangles quoting.
Librarian|One of six Home Librarian programs that must stay together -- its licence says so. Start here to search a catalogue; EditLibr edits one, Ascii2Libr builds one from text, Libr2Ascii dumps it back, PrintCards and PrintLabels print it. Manual in DOC/homelibr.
sox|Converts between audio formats. There is no sound device here, so it converts files rather than plays them. Sample .iff sounds are in DOC/sox. Needs Microware's cio.
p2c|Translates Pascal to C. It reads LIB/p2c/p2crc at startup and stops with "file not found" if that is missing; programs it emits must be linked against LIB/libp2c.l.
textb|An ASCII Mandelbrot viewer -- it asks four questions and draws. Try X_Coord -2.3, Y_Coord -2.0, RANGE 4.0, Max Iter 32. Needs Microware's cio.
xlate|Translates 6809 assembly source into 68000. Pairs with as09 (the 6809 assembler on this disk) and with `tab', which tabulates either dialect. Needs cio.
lwf|Turns plain text into PostScript, the way Unix enscript does. It reads /dd/USR/LIB/lwf.prologue and stops without it. No PostScript printer here, so send the output to a file and take it elsewhere.
ccheck|Checks C source for mismatched brackets, quotes and comment markers, and for indentation that disagrees with the nesting. Needs cio.
ub68kdemo|UniBasic 1.10, and it does compile -- the trick is that it runs its build through $SHELL. With SHELL unset it hunts for `/dd/bash' and dies with "Error Exit" and error 216. Do `setenv SHELL /dd/CMDS/sh', work in a directory holding basic.h and basic.l (DOC/unibasic has them), have your C toolchain reachable with CDEF and CLIB set, and give it memory. Verified end to end. DEMO VERSION: the symbol table is capped, nothing else is.
ob68kdemo|OmniBasic 1.16, same arrangement as ub68kdemo and the same SHELL trick -- see its entry. Run it from /dd/DOC/omnibasic. DEMO VERSION, capped symbol table.
sddemo|White's Speedisk 2.10 de-fragmenter, demo build. Wants an 80x24 screen and drops to tty mode without one.
dhry|Dhrystone 2.0. Twelve builds of the same source sit in CMDS/DHRY -- run several and compare, which is what tells you the compiler's cost. Under os9exec the number describes the host machine, not a 68000.
head|First lines of a file. This GNU build wants `head -n 20 file' -- the older `head -20' form is rejected as an unrecognized option. Needs cio.
tac|Prints a file backwards, last line first -- cat's mirror image. Needs cio.
as0|A 6800 cross-assembler, not a 68000 one -- as1 is 6801, as4 is 6804, as5 is 6805, as11 is 68HC11 and as09 is 6809. DOC/xasm/asm.doc is their manual; source for all of them is in SRC/xasm.
oleo|GNU Oleo, a spreadsheet. It needs a real terminal -- run it from a login so TERM is set, not from a bare shell. sc is the other spreadsheet on this disk; they are unrelated programs.
booz|Extracts and lists zoo archives. `booz -l file.zoo' to look, `booz -x' to extract. fiz repairs a zoo archive that will not open.
funzip|Unzips from a pipe rather than a file: `funzip < thing.zip > thing'. For a normal archive use unzip; zipinfo lists what is inside one.
gs403|Aladdin Ghostscript 4.03. Set GS_LIB first -- `setenv GS_LIB /dd/LIB/gs403' -- or it cannot find gs_init.ps and stops. Everything it needs, fonts included, is in that directory. The older gs33 on this disk has never had its support files.
hexbin|Decodes Macintosh BinHex (.hqx) files, which is how Mac software travelled by mail and BBS. binhex goes the other way; unsit opens StuffIt archives and macunpack opens PackIt ones. All trap-free. DOC/macutils has the package readme.
mgif|`mgif -i file.gif' inspects a GIF and prints its structure -- that works on any terminal. Displaying an image does not: it writes straight to Atari ST graphics memory. Try it on /dd/DEMO/gulls.gif.
tet|Draws the board and ignores the keyboard, and the reason is in its source: tet.c puts the terminal in raw mode inside `#ifndef OSK', so the OS-9 build has no terminal setup at all. Set the mode from outside before starting it (Microware's tmode), or rebuild with an OSK branch using _ss_opt -- LIB/alib.l provides both that and ioctl. Source in SRC/tet.
elvis|A full vi/ex clone, built here from the archive that was always on this disk. Needs TERM and TERMCAP set -- `SYS/login' does both. `view' opens read-only, REBUILT/vi.elvis is the same program as vi, and all of them need CMDS/elvis present because they exec it.
vi|One of three unrelated vi editors here, and the only one that is the genuine Berkeley ex/vi rather than a clone -- its source in SRC/effo_vi is the real ex_*.c files. `vi -x' becomes ex, `vi -d' becomes edit. DOC/README-VI compares all three.
vi_nocio|PVIC 1.0a, the smallest of the three vi editors on this disk, public domain, no source or docs here. DOC/README-VI compares it with vi and elvis.
